    "q-89400": {
      "dom_id": "step-q89400",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "In a coffee shop, $28%$ of the customers order their coffee to go. If $12$ customers from the coffee shop are randomly selected, what is the probability that $3$ or less will order their coffee to go? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of customers that order their coffee to go.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ customers\n\n- the probability that each customer orders the coffee to go is $p=28%=0.28$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,0.28).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(0.28)}^{x}{(1-0.28)}^{12-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(0.28)}^{x}{(0.72)}^{12-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting a \"success\" (i.e., the customer orders the coffee to go) on at most $3$ trials. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{0}){(0.28)}^{0}{(0.72)}^{12}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{1}){(0.28)}^{1}{(0.72)}^{11}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{2}){(0.28)}^{2}{(0.72)}^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{3}){(0.28)}^{3}{(0.72)}^{9} \\\\  & ={(0.28)}^{0}{(0.72)}^{12}+12{(0.28)}^{1}{(0.72)}^{11}+66{(0.28)}^{2}{(0.72)}^{10}+220{(0.28)}^{3}{(0.72)}^{9} \\\\  & =0.555\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
